{
 "Each peg is a farm in East Africa, placed by how much rain it gets and how high it is. Its colour is the crop that does best there: tea, maize or sorghum.": "4d7f7e4c68.mp3",
 "A decision tree starts with the single question that best separates the crops. Here it is: is the rain above about eight hundred millimetres? One cut, two boxes.": "f4df6ac09f.mp3",
 "Then each box asks its own next question. Is it high up? Tea grows in the cool, wet highlands. Three levels of questions, and the field is cut into neat boxes.": "7071f24ec1.mp3",
 "Check it on farms it never saw. Most land in the right box. White rings mark the mistakes.": "9c4efcd870.mp3",
 "Let the tree keep asking until every box is pure, and it carves tiny boxes around every odd farm. Perfect on what it learned, worse on new farms. It has memorised the noise.": "e78495bdb3.mp3",
 "So trees are kept short, or pruned, or many are averaged into a forest. A short tree is also easy to read: anyone can follow its questions.": "49c86cfb9f.mp3"
}
